WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.260
External commands receive arguments as separate values.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:09.240
A safe Bash script preserves those boundaries from input through invocation.

3
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:16.460
Problems appear when a script collapses values into one command string and asks the shell to interpret it later.

4
00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:23.600
That design mixes data, options, quoting rules, and shell syntax into something harder to review.

5
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.520
Quoting helps preserve a value as one argument.

6
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:34.700
A filename, identifier, label, or option value may contain spaces or characters that Bash would otherwise treat specially.

7
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.120
If the value should be one argument, the script should pass it as one argument.

8
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:44.660
Quoting is basic, but it is one of the most important reliability and security habits in Bash.

9
00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.260
Arrays are the preferred structure when a script needs to build an argument list.

10
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:55.040
Fixed options, validated option values, and operands can be separate array elements.

11
00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:00.580
The script can add optional supported arguments without turning the whole invocation into a shell string.

12
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:04.920
That makes behavior clearer and reduces accidental interpretation.

13
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:11.680
The form "$@" is important when forwarding an existing argument list from a wrapper or function.

14
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.700
Used correctly, it preserves each original argument boundary.

15
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:22.440
That matters when a wrapper receives values it should pass along as data rather than flatten into a string and split again.

16
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.640
Validation and argument preservation solve different problems.

17
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.540
Validation decides whether a value is acceptable for this workflow.

18
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:37.400
Quoting, arrays, and "$@" preserve that accepted value when invoking the process.

19
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.900
A defensive script uses both layers together: validate first, then pass values as structured arguments to known executables.
